CloudBurrow patch 107 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#2134).

The bug: a go-googlesql method copies the C++ pointers of its receiver and
arguments into its request, then waits for the module's lock. A handle whose
last Go use was that copy can be finalized, and its C++ object freed in the
engine's WebAssembly memory, before the call runs; the call then reads freed
memory. newSimpleCatalog passed its LanguageOptions inside
BuiltinFunctionOptions to AddGoogleSQLFunctions2 and never used it again: on
main CI (run 44954090598) a spec case's sql.Open hung for ten minutes inside
the analyzer, in absl's hash-set probe loop, which also stalled every other
goroutine behind the garbage collector's stop-the-world. Calling
AddGoogleSQLFunctions2 with a freed LanguageOptions reproduces the same loop.
The handles googlesqlite drops this way (the catalog's language options,
RegisterProto's FileDescriptorProto, and the collation helpers' annotation,
value and map proto) are now kept alive through the call with
runtime.KeepAlive.

The spec runner gets a per-test timeout (4 minutes, or
GOOGLESQLITE_PER_TEST_TIMEOUT), which names the overdue cases, and, when
GOOGLESQLITE_TEST_TRACE names a file, logs each case's start and end there,
so a hang that also freezes the watchdog still names its case.

